Feature: S107 owned-origin CORS migration rehearsal
  All policies, profiles and business data are fictional.
  The banner is exactly "Training rehearsal - synthetic data - not a live Azure result".
  One A-S107 satisfier owns this bundle; B-S107 and D-S107 remain separately gated.

  Scenario: A static entry cannot measure HTTP browser-origin enforcement
    Given the learner opens index.html directly from disk
    When the learner checks a prediction about an authored policy
    Then the education verifier can pass or fail
    But user impact and actual CORS remain unknown
    And the page provides explicit local Node startup instructions

  Scenario: Existing browser origins survive a compatible migration
    Given two owned HTTP frontends and an API listen only on literal 127.0.0.1 at actual ephemeral ports
    And a fresh sandboxed, signed Edge browser uses native HTTP without routing interception
    When each frontend requests baseline v1 and compatible v2 cross-origin
    Then both browsers can read the fictional profile
    And each server receipt records the actual Origin, HTTP 200 and matching allow-origin header

  Scenario: An incompatible candidate interrupts one existing origin without bypass
    Given both origins were valid existing v1 callers
    When frontend B requests the incompatible v2 policy
    Then the API actually sends HTTP 200 without an allow-origin header
    And Chromium blocks reading that response specifically because of CORS
    And the same-origin receipt contains metadata only, never the denied profile
    And frontend A can still read the incompatible candidate
    And B can read a compatible rollback without erasing its original interruption

  Scenario: CSP or missing transport evidence is not CORS denial
    When an attempt has a CSP violation or no matching finished HTTP receipt
    Then its user impact and CORS observation remain unknown
    And neither a model flag nor a generic fetch error qualifies as CORS proof

  Scenario: A previous document's receipt cannot masquerade as a new response
    Given an older document has a finished HTTP receipt
    And the API later becomes unavailable
    When a new document requests evidence
    Then its non-secret per-document correlation ID has no matching receipt
    And the old receipt remains retained without qualifying the new attempt
    And all issued IDs and response slots stay strictly bounded

  Scenario: History cannot conveniently evict failures
    Given a denied, a failed prediction or an unknown attempt is retained
    When the learner resets controls or completes later successful attempts
    Then the original attempts and their separate outcomes remain
    And a seventh attempt is rejected without changing the six-attempt history

  Scenario: Keyboard, invalid inputs and CSP are exercised in the actual DOM
    Then the core controls work with keyboard focus and activation
    And malformed conditions produce an accessible error without mutation
    And explicit reset restores controls but retains the journal
    And inline scripts and a disallowed owned-origin connection are blocked by CSP

  Scenario: Verification is isolated, bounded and read-only by default
    Given only the published Playwright 1.56.1 public cache digests and signed Edge 156 are selected
    Then no install, download, credentials, external request, unsafe security flag or shared helper edit occurs
    And all caller-owned listeners and browser resources close in finally
    And only literal owned scratch directories are removed after process exit
    And historical observations change only with exact CAPTURE_BROWSER_EVIDENCE=1 deliberate capture
    And raw CRLF source bytes and canonical Git bytes are qualified separately
    And broader lifecycle qualification and native Azure certification remain false
